// rustc borrowck/borrowed-data-escapes-closure-148392.rs — upstream E0521
// imported batch B167 @ da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (rust 1.100.0)
// shelf: admit · root bck.NEW-CESC — RE-PORTED AS-IS 2026-09-07 (stage 4)
// ── BUCKET 2: re-ported as-is, still admitted, still a defect. The old port had bound
//    the closure to a `let` and CALLED it; upstream leaves it as a bare statement
//    expression, and that form is admitted too.
// ── ROOT RE-CHECKED. `b`'s type carries a region minted OUTSIDE the closure; inside a
//    `move` closure `&a` names the closure's OWN copy of `a`, and assigning it into `b`
//    lets that region escape. Nothing constrains an escaping closure region here except
//    the `dyn` object-lifetime bound — MEASURED PAIR 2026-09-07:
//      Box::new(|| -> i64 { x })   coerced to Box<dyn Fn() -> i64>  -> REFUSED
//        "coercion to `dyn Fn` requires `|| -> i64: 'static` ... (object lifetime bound)"
//      keep(|| -> &i64 { &x })     through a plain generic `fn keep<F>(f:F)->F` -> ADMITS
//    One variable: the escape CHANNEL. This port uses neither channel — it escapes
//    through an assignment to a capture — so it lands on the unconstrained side.
// Original path: tests/ui/borrowck/borrowed-data-escapes-closure-148392.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 8-12 —
//     let a = 0;
//     let mut b = None;
//     move || {
//         b = Some(&a); //~ ERROR borrowed data escapes outside of closure
//     };
// Modifications:
//   · `let a = 0` / `let mut b = None` -> the same with types written out (`i64`,
//     `Option<&i64>`); Logos does not infer `b`'s type from the closure body
//   · `Some`/`None` -> `Option::Some`/`Option::None` (spelling)
//   · assert -> exit code: `fn main() -> i32`
package borrowed_data_escapes_closure_148392;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut b: Option<&i64> = Option::None;
    move || { b = Option::Some(&a); };
    return 0i32;
}
